---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

// THE DICTIONARY (Antonio's idea). Every term a DJ meets on this site or in a
// booth, one tap deep. Two rules:
//   1. Definitions come ONLY from material already verified on this site
//      (manufacturer docs, the facts files, the mixed-audience explainers).
//      Nothing is defined here that the site cannot back.
//   2. Same collapse pattern as the FAQ: the term list IS the index, the
//      reader expands what they came for.
// Terms are grouped the way a DJ meets them, not alphabetically: drives and
// formats, the library, the players, the mixer and sound.
const groups = [
  {
    title: 'Drives and formats',
    terms: [
      { t: 'FAT32', d: 'The file system that every Pioneer and AlphaTheta player reads, old and new, which is why this site recommends it. Its one real limit is 4GB per FILE, not per drive, and no DJ track approaches that.' },
      { t: 'exFAT', d: 'A newer file system. Officially supported on CDJ-3000, CDJ-3000X, XDJ-RX3, XDJ-XZ and XDJ-AZ, refused by every CDJ-2000 including the NXS2. Most drives over 32GB ship with it from the factory, which is why a brand-new stick can fail on an older player out of the box. rekordbox writes exFAT to USB drives only, never SD cards.' },
      { t: 'NTFS', d: 'A Windows file system. No player reads it, and rekordbox will not export to it. If a drive is NTFS, it gets reformatted before it ever sees a booth.' },
      { t: 'MBR (Master Boot Record)', d: 'One of two ways a drive describes its own layout, and the one players expect. AlphaTheta documents that the CDJ-3000 does not support the GUID scheme, so when a format tool asks, pick MBR. On a Mac this means erasing the DEVICE, not the volume, in Disk Utility.' },
      { t: 'GPT (GUID Partition Table)', d: 'The other layout scheme, and a recurring cause of "it formatted fine but the player refuses it". You get GPT by accident on a Mac when you erase the volume instead of the device.' },
      { t: 'Quick format vs full format', d: 'Quick format rebuilds the drive’s index and is fine on a healthy drive. A full format writes to every cell, which makes it a stress test: it is what you run on a SUSPECT drive, and a drive that fails one is retired, not trusted with a gig.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'The library',
    terms: [
      { t: 'rekordbox database', d: 'The hidden index rekordbox writes onto your USB next to the music: playlists, cues, grids, waveforms. When a player mounts your drive but shows no playlists, this is usually what died or mismatched, not your music. The audio files sit untouched in their folders.' },
      { t: 'Device Library', d: 'The classic database format rekordbox exports to USB, the one older CDJs and XDJs read.' },
      { t: 'OneLibrary (Device Library Plus)', d: 'The newer database format, two names for the same thing, read by CDJ-3000X, XDJ-AZ, OPUS-QUAD and OMNIS-DUO. Since rekordbox 7.2.13 a standard export writes BOTH formats to the stick, which is why this site anchors on that version: one export, every player.' },
      { t: 'EXPORT mode', d: 'The rekordbox mode that prepares USB drives. Officially free, no subscription, confirmed by rekordbox’s own plans FAQ. PERFORMANCE mode is the other one, for playing from the laptop.' },
      { t: 'FOLDER view', d: 'The player’s file browser. It reads your audio files directly, folder by folder, ignoring the database entirely. You lose playlists, cues and prepared waveforms; you keep the music and the gig. Move one of the emergency list for a reason.' },
      { t: 'Library backup', d: 'File > Library > Backup Library in rekordbox 7, to a different drive or cloud. The USB in your pocket is an export, not a backup: lose the laptop library and the cues and playlists on it are the only copy left.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'The players',
    terms: [
      { t: 'PRO DJ LINK', d: 'The network that connects players and mixer. Over LINK, any player can browse and load from a drive that is physically in another player, officially supported since the CDJ-900/2000 generation. If your drive dies mid-set, the drive in the other deck is still reachable.' },
      { t: 'USB STOP', d: 'The button you hold before pulling a drive. The manual is blunt: removing a drive (or cutting power) while the USB indicator is blinking can delete management data or make the drive unreadable. Every time, both directions: player and rekordbox.' },
      { t: 'Emergency Loop', d: 'Official AlphaTheta behaviour, and officially named: when a track ends and no next track is loaded, the player loops the last beats instead of going silent. If you hear it, the player is doing its job; load something.' },
      { t: 'E-8302 (CANNOT PLAY TRACK)', d: 'CDJ-3000 and CDJ-3000X error meaning the track data on the drive cannot be read correctly. The official response: try the track on another player, and re-import the file to the drive if possible. Usually a corrupt export or wrong file system, not dead hardware.' },
      { t: 'HID mode', d: 'The mode where a player becomes a controller for software on a laptop instead of playing from its own drive. A booth surprise on older players: not all of them support it for every software.' },
      { t: 'Firmware', d: 'The player’s own software, and part of your rig whether you like it or not. Known freezes and dropouts have official fixed-in versions; check the version in UTILITY at soundcheck. And never update anything, yours or the booth’s, on gig day.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'The mixer and the sound',
    terms: [
      { t: 'TRIM / GAIN', d: 'Two names for the same top-of-strip knob: how loud the source arrives in the channel, before the fader. First suspect when one channel is quiet with the fader up.' },
      { t: 'Crossfader assign', d: 'The per-channel switch (A / THRU / B) deciding whether the crossfader controls that channel. Assigned to the wrong side, the channel is silent with every fader up: the number one silent-channel cause. On OPUS-QUAD and XDJ-AZ it lives in the on-screen settings, not on a physical switch.' },
      { t: 'CUE / PFL', d: 'The DJ’s word and the tech’s word for the same thing: listening to a channel in your headphones before it reaches the room. If your headphones are silent, check the MIX knob is toward CUE, not MASTER, before blaming the jack.' },
      { t: 'BOOTH output', d: 'The mixer’s second output, feeding the DJ’s own monitors, separate from MASTER which feeds the room. Two knobs, two destinations. In a pinch the booth output pair can substitute for a dead master pair.' },
      { t: 'Master insert', d: 'A patch point on the back of some mixers where an outboard processor sits in the master signal. If something is plugged in and nothing sends audio back, the master goes silent with every fader up. If you find one patched, it is almost certainly the venue’s: ask before pulling it.' },
      { t: 'Signal chain', d: 'The ordered path audio takes after your mixer: house processor, amplifiers, speakers. Past the booth output it belongs to the venue, and the fastest fix is the exact sentence: "Mixer master is up, master meters are moving. Nothing after the booth output."' },
    ],
  },
];

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'DefinedTermSet',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/knowledge/dictionary#terms',
  name: 'The DJ reliability dictionary',
  hasDefinedTerm: groups.flatMap((g) => g.terms.map((x) => ({
    '@type': 'DefinedTerm', name: x.t, description: x.d,
  }))),
};
---
<Base
  title="The DJ Dictionary - MBR, OneLibrary, Emergency Loop, Explained | Save My Gig!"
  description="Every term that decides whether your USB plays: FAT32, MBR, OneLibrary, PRO DJ LINK, Emergency Loop, crossfader assign. Plain definitions, manufacturer-verified."
  path="/knowledge/dictionary"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/knowledge">Knowledge base</a> / Dictionary</p>
    <div class="status-line">DICTIONARY</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:0.8rem">The words that <span class="accent">save gigs</span></h1>
    <p class="dim" style="max-width:42rem;margin-bottom:2rem;line-height:1.6">
      Every term on this site and in the booth, one tap deep. Defined from
      manufacturer documentation and the facts this site has verified, nothing
      folklore.
    </p>

    {groups.map((g) => (
      <section class="dict-group">
        <h2 class="dict-h">{g.title}</h2>
        {g.terms.map((x) => (
          <details class="panel dict-term">
            <summary>
              <h3 class="dict-t">{x.t}</h3>
              <span class="dict-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="dim dict-d">{x.d}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </section>
    ))}

    <p class="dim" style="margin-top:2rem;font-size:0.9rem">
      Missing a word that confused you in a booth? <a href="/feedback">Tell us</a>
      and it gets added.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .crumb { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--text-faint); margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .crumb a { color: var(--text-dim); text-decoration: none; }
  .crumb a:hover { color: var(--red); }
  .dict-group { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .dict-h {
    font-size: 0.95rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--edge); padding-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .dict-term { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; padding: 0.95rem 1.15rem; }
  .dict-term > summary {
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem;
    cursor: pointer; list-style: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
  }
  .dict-term > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .dict-term > summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: -2px; }
  .dict-t { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 0; letter-spacing: -0.01em; text-transform: none; }
  .dict-chev {
    flex: 0 0 auto; width: 8px; height: 8px; margin-top: -2px;
    border-right: 2px solid var(--text-dim); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--text-dim);
    transform: rotate(45deg); transition: transform 0.2s ease;
  }
  .dict-term[open] > summary .dict-chev { transform: rotate(225deg); }
  .dict-term[open] > summary { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .dict-d { margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .dict-chev { transition: none; } }
</style>
